## 0.5 Quick start with `./andes-sim`

`./andes-sim` is the canonical end-user entry point (the C++ driver; it
self-builds on first use). It wraps the developer-grade scripts
documented in Sections 1-5 (`setup.sh`, `tests/e2e.sh`,
`scripts/run_e2e.sh`) behind a few subcommands.

Four-command worked example - cold checkout to passing suite:

```sh
./andes-sim build                                       # qemu, vsim, plugin, simpoint, fixtures
./andes-sim list engines                                # discover the built engines
./andes-sim run --model hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium \
  tests/fixtures/rt_c_hello.elf                         # one CSR-trigger handoff
./andes-sim test                                        # full e2e suite (defaults: MODE=both, JOBS=8)
```

Expected last line of `./andes-sim test`:

```
PASS 45, FAIL 0, SKIP 0 (elapsed: <Ns>)
```

Subcommand cheat sheet:

| Command                                                          | What it does                                                        |
|------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `./andes-sim list <models|engines>`                              | List the 3 models, or the discovered engines with their hardware params (XLEN/VLEN/DLEN/ELEN; `-` = not reported). |
| `./andes-sim build [--what TARGET] [--dry-run]`                  | Build artifacts; targets: `all`, `qemu`, `vsim`, `plugin`, `simpoint`, `fixtures`, `runtime`. |
| `./andes-sim run --model fast|cycle|hybrid [--trigger marker|pc|icount] [--engine ID] <elf>` | Run one ELF on a model (hybrid = QEMU -> vsim handoff). |
| `./andes-sim test [--filter REGEX] [--mode marker|pc|both] [--jobs N]` | Run the e2e suite; defaults `--mode both --jobs 8`.              |

Run `./andes-sim <cmd> -h` for the full flag list of a subcommand (set
`ANDES_SIM_DEV=1` to see the developer commands). The SimPoint pipeline
(`run --mode simpoint`, `estimate`, `profile`, `cluster`, `archive`,
`replay`) still lives in the Python reference driver `./hsim.py` until
the C++ port lands. The rest of this guide (Sections 1-7) documents the
underlying scripts and is useful when you need to bypass `./andes-sim`
(custom env vars, single-leg debugging, alternative CPU YAML, etc.).

