## 3. Switching modes - which trigger to use

| Mode     | Profiles          | Switch boundary                                          | Cycle figure           |
|----------|-------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------|
| marker   | a kernel          | `slti x0,x0,0x5A0/1` insn match (markers in source)         | kernel cycles          |
| PC       | a kernel          | PC equals a boundary: any symbol or `0x` address (both `--pc-start`/`--pc-end` required) | kernel cycles |
| icount   | a sampled window  | N retired insns (single boundary; no markers)            | window cycles          |
| SimPoint | the whole program | clustering picks representative slices, each measured like icount | whole-program estimate |

CSR/PC delimit ONE kernel you choose; icount/SimPoint sample the whole
program by instruction count (SimPoint is icount repeated, weight-combined).
How each is driven: marker/pc/icount go through `./andes-sim run --model
hybrid --trigger marker|pc|icount`; SimPoint still goes through the Python
reference (`./hsim.py run --mode simpoint`, also exposed as
`./hsim.py estimate`). The cycle figure is always
the vsim `mcycle` delta (offset 624); SimPoint extrapolates many deltas
into `estimated_cycles`.

Decision flow:

```
Profiling ONE region you can name (a kernel)?
  binary is yours to edit          -> marker (andes_sim_roi_begin()/end() around it)
  not editable, symbol/PC at ends  -> PC   (./andes-sim run --model hybrid
                                            --trigger pc --pc-start S --pc-end E)
Profiling the WHOLE program (or a CSR-less blob)?
  one window by instruction count  -> icount
  representative phases, combined  -> SimPoint  (./hsim.py run --mode simpoint)
```

**Hardware-config sync.** A hybrid run models ONE core on two engines, so
the QEMU fast leg inherits the selected engine's hardware config: its
`-cpu` VLEN comes from the engine's `--describe` (512 when the engine
reports none) and shows in the run banner as `vlen=N`. An engine whose
XLEN is not 64 (e.g. `vsim:d23`, rv32) cannot share architectural state
with the rv64 fast leg and is rejected with a clear error.

Supported VLEN range: 128 to 1024 bits. The handoff state file reserves
128 bytes per vector register (`HYBRID_VLEN_BYTES_MAX`); an engine whose
VLEN exceeds 1024 is refused with a clear error instead of silently
truncating the V registers. For a standalone fast run the built-in CPU is
`vlen=512`; override it through the passthrough tail, e.g.
`andes-sim run --model fast app.elf -- -cpu andes-ax45mpv,vlen=1024`
(the last `-cpu` wins).

**Window boundary semantics.** Markers are HINT NOPs: the window is the
span strictly between them. A pc/icount boundary is real user code: the
instruction at `--pc-start` (or the N-th instruction for icount) executes
INSIDE the measured window -- the fast leg stops just before it and vsim
resumes at it, so no instruction is lost across the handoff. The
instruction at `--pc-end` is where vsim drains.

> **Writing a kernel for cycle measurement (marker/PC modes):** keep the measured
> kernel anchored inside the `slti x0,x0,0x5A0/1` span -- read its inputs and
> write its outputs through memory inside the span, or wrap the kernel in a
> `__attribute__((noinline))` function called between the markers. The
> markers' `"memory"` clobber pins memory I/O but not register-only compute,
> so a kernel that carries live vector values in registers across the
> boundary may be hoisted/sunk out of the measured window. See
> `tests/fixtures/runtime/rt_c_helpers.h`.

---

