### 4.5 Warmup window - drop cold-cache cycles from the report

Every cycle figure above (marker / pc / icount / simpoint) treats the entire
vsim window as the measured region. The first few thousand vsim insns hit
cold caches/BPU/TLB and run slower than steady state; if you only care
about the warm-cache cost, those cycles are noise. Pass
`--vsim-warmup-icount K` to anchor a measurement-start snapshot K committed
insns after vsim entry. The orchestrator then reports a 3-way split:

```
warmup:    K_cycles cycles   K_insns insns      (cold prefix)
measure:   M_cycles cycles   M_insns insns      (warm tail, this is the
                                                 ROI-final figure)
total:     T_cycles cycles   T_insns insns      (sum, matches the no-warmup
                                                 single figure)
```

Mechanism: the snapshot stays inside one vsim process so caches do not
get flushed - only the stats window moves. K=0 (default) reproduces the
no-warmup behaviour bit-for-bit. The K-th-commit boundary is captured via
a Debug-Module halt + abstract-command read of `mcycle` / `minstret` at
the moment the counter trips, then vsim resumes; in CSR / PC mode the
snapshot lands on exactly the K-th retire, in icount mode it can be off
by a handful of insns because ResumeDriver injects Program-Buffer ops
that bump the hart-side minstret without going through the trigger.

```sh
./andes-sim run tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul.elf \
  --model hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --vsim-warmup-icount 1000

./andes-sim run tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul_free.elf \
  --model hybrid --trigger icount --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --qemu-icount 2000 --vsim-icount 5000 \
  --vsim-warmup-icount 1000     # require K < --vsim-icount
```

The warmup pair lands in the drained state file at the F13 ABI offsets
`mcycle_at_measure_start` (offset 5440) and `minstret_at_measure_start`
(offset 5448); both remain zero when K=0 or the workload exited inside
the warmup window. The marker/pc e2e wrapper prints a
`[<fixture> warmup] K=K mcycle_at_measure_start=...` line; the
orchestrator's `run` subcommand prints the 3-way breakdown per step.

Coming from gem5: this is the analogue of `--warmup-insts N` followed by
an implicit `m5.stats.reset()`. The constraint differs - QEMU has no
cache model, so unlike gem5 the warmup MUST happen inside the cycle-
accurate phase (vsim). Otherwise the contract is identical: K cold
insns, then the measured window.

### 4.6 Capturing a Kanata pipeline trace of the ROI

When debugging a hot path on vsim, pass `--kanata FILE` to `./andes-sim run`
in marker or pc mode. The resulting trace contains pipeline records only
for the ROI region (between QEMU 1's drain and vsim's drain) -- vsim
boot and DMI resume overhead are filtered out.

```sh
# marker mode: the ROI is the span between the two ROI markers (slti x0,x0,0x5A0/1)
# markers already in the source. vsim auto-derives the window.
./andes-sim run tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul.elf \
  --model hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --kanata /tmp/roi.kanata

# pc mode: the ROI bounds are whatever PCs you passed.
./andes-sim run tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul_free.elf \
  --model hybrid --trigger pc --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --pc-start vmatmul_kernel --pc-end 0x1002cc \
  --kanata /tmp/roi.kanata
```

Add `--objdump FILE` so the kanata `L` records embed the disassembly
text next to each PC. Generate the dump once with the toolchain
`objdump -d`:

```sh
$HYBRID_TOOLCHAIN/bin/riscv64-unknown-elf-objdump -d \
  tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul.elf > /tmp/roi.S
./andes-sim run tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul.elf \
  --model hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --kanata /tmp/roi.kanata --objdump /tmp/roi.S
```

Open `/tmp/roi.kanata` in [Konata](https://github.com/shioyadan/Konata)
to view the pipeline.

Constraints:

- `--kanata` is only valid with `--model hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium` or `--model hybrid --trigger pc --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium`. icount and
  simpoint modes have no single ROI-exit PC; they are rejected with
  rc=2.
- marker mode requires exactly one `slti x0,x0,0x5A1` (exit marker) in the
  ELF. If the binary has more than one, vsim errors out with
  `multiple ... markers in ELF -- use --model hybrid --trigger pc --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium with explicit --pc-end`.
  Switch to pc mode and pick the desired exit explicitly.
- `--objdump` requires `--kanata`.

How it works:

- hsim forwards `KANATA_FILE` (and optionally `OBJDUMP_FILE`) to
  `tests/e2e.sh`, which appends `--kanata FILE --kanata-roi`
  (and `--objdump FILE`) to vsim's argv.
- vsim's `--kanata-roi` flag defers the kanata enable until after
  `--resume-from-qemu` loads the input state and `enable_hybrid_handoff`
  scans the ELF, then derives a one-shot `LoopGate`:
  - `start_pc` = the resumed PC (where vsim first fetches user code)
  - `end_pc`   = `--hybrid-exit-pc` (pc mode) or the unique
    `slti x0,x0,0x5A1` PC from CsrTrigger (marker mode)
  - `skip_iters=0`, `record_iters=1` (open on first fetch of start_pc,
    close on first retire of end_pc).

### 4.7 Verifying a hybrid run against a QEMU oracle (`--verify`)

With the QEMU2 handback removed, the code after your ROI never runs, so a
hybrid run normally proves only that a cycle figure came out. `--verify`
(default OFF) cross-checks the run's correctness:

```sh
./andes-sim run --model hybrid --trigger marker --verify your_prog.elf
# ...
# [marker window_cycles] 46  (vsim mcycle=... - qemu mcycle=...)
# [state-diff] PASS: 109 fields match (counters and passthrough CSRs excluded)
# [verify] PASS: vsim drain matches the QEMU oracle
```

How it works: after the normal run, the same ELF re-runs on pure QEMU (the
oracle) with the plugin draining at the ROI exit boundary, sampled at the
exact pc where vsim halted. The pre-ROI path is QEMU in both runs, so the
ROI inputs are identical -- any architectural divergence inside the ROI
(RTL bug, state-restore bug, drain bug) shows up as a field mismatch and
the run exits non-zero, listing each differing field:

```
[state-diff] x10: ref=0x3d got=0x0
[state-diff] FAIL: 1 mismatch(es) (109 fields compared)
```

What is compared: pc, all GPRs, all FPRs (when FP state is live), the V
CSRs and full vector registers (when V state is live). What is not:

- `mcycle`/`minstret` -- the engines count differently by design;
- the M-mode CSRs and PMP -- vsim's drain copies them from QEMU1's entry
  snapshot (the trap-free ROI cannot change them), so a compare would be
  meaningless.

Costs and limits: the oracle leg is one extra QEMU run of the whole
program (MHz-class -- cheap next to the vsim leg). `--verify` works with
`--trigger marker|pc`, assumes the single-shot ROI the hybrid run itself
assumes, and requires `--model hybrid` (the other models have nothing to
cross-check).

### 4.8 Localizing a divergence with checkpoint co-sim (`--cosim`, dev)

`--verify` tells you THAT the vsim leg diverged; `--cosim` tells you
roughly WHERE. It is a developer tool (hidden from the release help, like
`state-diff`): every N retired instructions inside the ROI, the vsim leg
halts via DMI, drains a resumable checkpoint to `<state>.ckpt<k>`, and
continues. The driver then re-runs the ELF on pure QEMU with the plugin
draining at the SAME retire counts, and diffs checkpoint k against
checkpoint k (same compare policy as `state-diff`):

```sh
ANDES_SIM_DEV=1 ./andes-sim run --model hybrid --trigger marker \
    --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --cosim --cosim-interval 500 my.elf
# ...
# [cosim] PASS: 3 checkpoint(s) + final state match the QEMU oracle (interval 500, 1692 ROI retires)
```

On a divergence, the first mismatching checkpoint brackets the bug:

```
[state-diff] x12: ref=0x2a got=0x2b
[cosim] FAIL: divergence in window 2 (retires 500..1000 after entry)
```

Re-run with a smaller `--cosim-interval` to shrink the window.

Alignment is by retire count, not pc: vsim restores `minstret` from the
entry state, so `checkpoint.minstret - entry.minstret` is the exact number
of post-entry retires (vsim's DMI halt overshoots a few instructions; the
overshoot is simply part of the measured delta, and the oracle counts to
the same number). In pc-trigger mode the boundary instruction itself
retires inside the window and is counted as retire #1 on both sides.

Costs and limits:
- `--trigger marker|pc` only, `--model hybrid` only, exclusive with
  `--verify` (the final-state check is included).
- No cycle figure: each checkpoint costs a DMI halt + drain + resume on
  the vsim leg, which pollutes the vsim `mcycle` delta, so `--cosim`
  suppresses the `window_cycles` line entirely. Re-run without `--cosim`
  to measure.
- Divergence is localized to an N-instruction window, not an exact
  instruction; intervals comparable to the halt overshoot (tens of
  instructions) are not meaningful.
- Memory is NOT compared -- only the architectural register state at each
  checkpoint (a memory bug surfaces indirectly, when a wrong value is
  loaded into a register).

